# Project Context — AI-Assisted Red Teaming Framework ("Red Team Control Center")

Human-in-the-loop security assessment platform: operator imports a client engagement
letter (PDF/DOCX/MD/TXT), the framework parses scope/targets/rules deterministically,
drafts a policy-checked command plan, runs each scanner command ONLY after explicit
human approval, correlates findings, and emits an HTML report. Authorized lab use only.

## Architecture map

- `frontend/src/App.jsx` — the ENTIRE single-page React 19 UI (one file). `App.css` styles it.
- `backend/main.py` — FastAPI app: targets, assessments, execute, analyze, report,
  settings, `/engagement/parse`, `/requirements/extract`, `/health`.
- `backend/models.py` — Pydantic request schemas with bounds.
- `backend/database.py` — SQLAlchemy/SQLite ORM + migrations.
- `backend/modules/` — `engagement_parser` (letter → structured brief, deterministic),
  `planner` (AI or default plan, policy-filtered), `policy_engine` (allowlist, fail-closed,
  scope checks — THE security core), `executor` (async subprocess, 360s timeout, output
  caps, env sandbox), `analyzer` (findings + scoring), `reporter` (Jinja2 HTML),
  `secret_store` (Fernet encryption at rest).
- `docker-compose.yml` — backend :8000, frontend :5173; Juice Shop :3000 and DVWA :8080
  behind the `lab` profile (see "Deployment modes").
- Sample letter: `JuiceBox_Security_Assessment_Request.pdf` (targets the two lab machines).
- `backend/smoke_e2e.py` — one-shot end-to-end run against the live stack (letter → target
  → plan → execute every step → analyze → report), printing each result.

## Run / test

- Demo stack: `docker compose --profile lab up --build` → UI at http://localhost:5173
- Toolkit only (no lab targets): `docker compose up --build`
- Frontend checks: `cd frontend; npm run lint; npm run build`
- Backend tests: `cd backend; .\venv\Scripts\python.exe -m pytest -q` (99 tests)
- End-to-end: `.\backend\venv\Scripts\python.exe backend\smoke_e2e.py` (stack must be up)
- PowerShell gotcha: the path contains `[IMPLEMENTATION]`, so always use
  `Set-Location -LiteralPath "..."` (plain `cd` treats brackets as wildcards).

## Key decisions & gotchas

- CORS default allows BOTH `http://localhost:5173` and `http://127.0.0.1:5173`
  (port-forward links hand out 127.0.0.1). Override via `CORS_ALLOW_ORIGINS`.
- Frontend `request()` helper: AbortController timeouts (75s general, 400s for execute —
  above the executor's 360s cap so the server decides how commands end), friendly
  network errors, FormData bodies skip the JSON header.
- UI polls `/health` every 10s → offline banner + auto-refresh on recovery.
- One global `busy` flag disables all buttons during requests; buttons label themselves
  ("Analyzing…", "Running…") so long requests read as progress, not dead UI.
- Scanner binaries (nmap, nuclei, …) exist ONLY inside the backend Docker image — a bare
  local Windows backend can drive UI/parser/planning but execution fails with a clear error.
- AI provider (OpenAI-compatible) is configured in the UI Configuration panel, stored
  Fernet-encrypted in the DB; never via env vars. Without it: deterministic fallbacks.
- Secrets are write-only: API responses return booleans, never values.
- Client letter restrictions (per-target tool bans) outrank both AI and operator plans.
- SQLite DB + secret key persist in the `data_volume` Docker volume (`down` keeps it,
  `down -v` wipes it).

## Phase-2 capabilities (current)

- Default deterministic plan: **7 policy-legal steps** (nmap/traceroute/dig/curl/whatweb/
  sslscan/nuclei), rate-limited (`--max-rate 30`, `-rl 30`), targets formatted as
  host:port with the scheme added exactly once.
- Deterministic analyzer: **per-tool parsers** (nmap service lines, nuclei template
  lines, curl header blocks incl. per-header missing-security-header findings +
  cookie flags, whatweb plugins, sslscan deprecated protocols) with detailed
  explanations, endpoint/parameter, distinct scoring drivers, concrete remediation.
- Finding rows persist endpoint/parameter/exploitability/impact/exposure; assessments
  persist `analysis_mode`; both surfaced in UI and report.
- Live execution terminal: executor keeps an in-memory capped buffer per in-flight
  execution (`live_registry`), `GET /assessments/{id}/executions/{eid}/live` streams
  it, frontend polls every 1 s and renders a black terminal panel in the step card.
- Report: executive summary + severity bar, analysis-mode statement, remediation
  summary table ordered by priority, scoring breakdown per finding, audit output,
  print stylesheet (Ctrl+P → clean PDF).
- Compose profiles: lab targets behind `--profile lab`; plain `docker compose up` is
  the real-world assessment toolkit. Backend no longer depends_on the lab targets.
- Letter v2 (`JB/SEC/2026/021`): criticalities 90/45, 7 objectives, deny-list
  (no traceroute on storefront) + allow-list (DVWA) restrictions, rate-limit rules,
  automation-benefit deliverable.

## Deployment modes (real-world vs. lab)

- The framework is an **assessor's toolkit**, not a lab-only exhibit. `docker compose up`
  runs backend + frontend only; the operator registers whatever targets the client
  letter authorizes. `--profile lab` adds Juice Shop + DVWA for the demo.
- Reachability is the only network requirement: scanners run inside the backend
  container, so the target must be routable from there (Docker DNS name, LAN IP, public
  host, or `host.docker.internal` for the Windows/macOS host itself). On Linux,
  uncomment `network_mode: host` on the backend for accurate LAN subnet scans.
- Proven out of the Docker network: `host.docker.internal` registered as a target,
  one step approved, real output in the live terminal.
- Targets arrive either from the parsed letter ("Set up everything from this letter")
  or by manual registration; either way the policy engine still gates every command
  against the authorized scopes and the letter's per-target tool restrictions.
- Remote/LAN UI access: set `VITE_API_URL` (browser → backend) and
  `CORS_ALLOW_ORIGINS` (backend → allowed origin) to the machine's LAN IP.
- Honest scope statement: non-destructive discovery/enumeration plus governance
  (authorization, approval, audit) by design — not an exploitation framework. That
  restraint is what makes it safe to point at a paying client's production estate.

## Manual effort vs. the framework (the demo's argument)

- Manual: read the letter, hand-translate 7 objectives into ~15–20 commands, remember
  each target's tool restrictions, run them one by one, then spend hours correlating
  output into scored findings and writing the report.
- Framework: letter parsed in seconds, restricted steps dropped automatically with the
  reason shown, 7 approvals, correlated + scored findings, report generated in minutes —
  with a full audit trail no manual run produces for free.
- The letter's final deliverable asks for exactly this evidence, so the comparison is
  part of the engagement, not a marketing aside.

## Fixed bugs (do not reintroduce)

- Parser: reportlab bullets extract via pypdf as `\x7f` (DEL); `BULLET_RE` must match
  it or objectives vanish and intro sentences pollute bullet lists. Items start only
  on bullet lines.
- Planner: never re-prefix a web target — `http://{target}` template + pre-prefixed
  target once produced `http://http://host:3000`.
- Planner: name-resolving tools (nmap/traceroute/dig/nslookup, `HOST_ONLY_TOOLS`) get the
  BARE host; nmap learns the port via `-p`. `nmap juice-shop:3000` and
  `dig +short juice-shop:3000` fail with "Failed to resolve" **and still exit 0**, so a
  whole plan once ran green and produced findings from curl alone. sslscan and the web
  tools parse `host:port` themselves and keep it.
- Analyzer: nuclei severity is compared LOWERCASE. Title-casing it first meant no driver
  branch ever matched, so a Critical CVE match scored the same as an informational one;
  `info` also maps to Low, never Medium.
- Analyzer: `_finding_curl` files header findings only after a real `HTTP/x nnn` status
  line (`STATUS_LINE_RE`), resetting per redirect hop. Without that gate a refused
  connection produced all five "missing security header" findings for a host that was
  never reached (main.py passes no return_code, so the status line is the only signal).
- Analyzer: every stream goes through `strip_ansi` before parsing. whatweb/sslscan/nuclei
  colourise even into a pipe, and the escapes land mid-token (`SSLv3 \x1b[32menabled`,
  `\x1b[1mHTTPServer\x1b[0m[nginx]`), which silently zeroed three parsers. The plan also
  passes `--color=never` / `--no-colour` / `-nc`, but parsing must not depend on it.
- Analyzer: nuclei v3 prints `[template] [http] [severity] url` — the protocol field is
  optional in `NUCLEI_LINE_RE`. nmap's tentative `ppp?` must still yield a finding
  (lower confidence, description says tentative), not be dropped by the regex.
- nuclei is pinned to 3.11.1 in the Dockerfile: 3.1.9 died with `fatal error: concurrent
  map writes` mid-run (exit 2, no findings, 149 s wasted). Do not downgrade.
- Executor timeout is 360 s, measured against the nuclei step: 5,242 templates /
  12,078 requests ≈ 308 s at the letter-mandated 30 req/s. A 300 s cap killed the scan
  at 99% complete and stamped every run "timed out". The plan passes `-duc` so runtime
  cannot creep past the cap via template auto-updates — which requires the Dockerfile
  to seed templates at build time (`nuclei -duc -update-templates`), or a fresh
  container dies with "no templates provided for scan". Re-measure before raising
  `-rl` or the timeout.

- Dropzone: whole box is the picker (click + Enter/Space via ref); hidden file input
  clears `e.target.value` after every pick so re-selecting the same file re-fires onChange.
- Report `window.open` runs after an await → popup blockers swallow it; fallback link
  (`reportUrl` state) is rendered when `window.open` returns null.
- Assessment rows are busy-guarded; execute/analyze/report capture the assessment id up
  front so switching assessments mid-request cannot cross-write results.
- Uploads go through `request()` (timeouts + friendly errors), not raw `fetch`.

## Demo runbook

1. `docker compose down -v` (clean DB) then `docker compose --profile lab up --build`
2. Open http://localhost:5173 → import the PDF in "Read the client's letter"
3. "Set up everything from this letter" → review the plan
4. Demo policy rejection: edit a command dangerously (e.g. `nmap -oN out.txt ...` or
   retarget to google.com) → approve → refused with a clear reason
5. Approve & execute each step → live terminal streams output → audit trail keeps it
6. Analyze results → scored findings → Generate report (HTML, cites the engagement)
7. Real-world proof: add `host.docker.internal:<port>` as a target and approve a step —
   the scanners reach a machine outside the compose network (nmap resolves it to the
   host's IP, traceroute shows the hop through the Docker gateway).

Two API gates that look like bugs during a demo but are deliberate: the plan is frozen
once any step has executed, and analysis requires every *enabled* step to have run
(disable steps before starting if you want a shorter run).